  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Compound Chain Rule: Decide the Direction First",
    "description": "Handle several changing quantities by marking each as direct or inverse to the target.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach one question at a time around the target variable. For each changing factor, ask: if only this factor increased, would the target increase or decrease? Mark it direct or inverse, predict the overall direction, then multiply scale factors. Progress from two factors to three and four factors, including output, cost, earnings and lamps. Close with a symbolic chain-rule relation.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach the chapter's chain-rule remark explicitly: compare every changing item with the quantity being found. Build a direction table mentally: if increasing a factor would increase the target, use it directly; if it would decrease the target, use it inversely. Cover two-factor and three-factor cases, earnings, output, lamps/cost, and symbolic proportional reasoning. The learner should be able to predict whether the answer rises or falls before calculating and use that prediction as a check."
  },
  "scriptId": "cr-03-compound-chain-rule-directions",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "schemaVersion": 1
